#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f0213e01-520f-5b59-96d3-1d94a50deb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12591" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t)$, we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$, $\\mathbf r'(1)$, and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1,-2, 2t⟩ \\\\\nr^{′}(1) &= ⟨1,-2, 2⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(1), \\frac{d}{dt}(-2), 2\\frac{d}{dt}(t)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 0, 2⟩ \\\\\nr^{″}(1) &= ⟨0, 0, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1) &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&=-4i - 2j \\\\\n‖r^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1)‖ &= \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} + (-2)^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{20} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(1) &= (‖r^{′}(1) \\cdot r^{″}(1)‖)/(‖r^{′}(1)‖^{3}) = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3^{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119441 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "b17659af-7eb8-5922-a8b7-ab1e9dcfda08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12592" :question/problem "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t)$, we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + t^{2})⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 2t⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{2^{2} + (2t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4 + 4t^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t)⟩ = ⟨0, 2⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t) &= ⟨2, 2t, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{vmatrix}2t \\\\ 0\\end{vmatrix}2k \\\\\n&= 4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖)/(‖r^{′}(t)‖^{3}) = (4)/((2\\sqrt{1 + t^{2}})^{3}) = (1)/(2(1 + t^{2})^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "0ad622cf-852f-5795-81ec-d147f050074e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12608" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t$." :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(t^{2}), \\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(1)⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2t, 2, 0⟩ \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(2t)^{2} + (2)^{2} + 0^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4t^{2} + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4(t^{2} + 1)} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{″}(t) &= ⟨\\frac{d}{dt}(2t), \\frac{d}{dt}(2), \\frac{d}{dt}(0)⟩ = ⟨2, 0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t) &= ⟨2t, 2, 0⟩ \\cdot ⟨2, 0, 0⟩ \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\\n&=-4k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖ &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nκ(t) &= (‖r^{′}(t) \\cdot r^{″}(t)‖)/(‖r^{′}(t)‖^{3}) = (4)/(8(t^{2} + 1)^{3/2}) = (1)/(2(t^{2} + 1)^{3/2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "920d9611-7181-5210-969c-984452d0ea8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117911} {:answer/id #uuid "d47e86f1-d930-582a-a8a5-1ad492ca8fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117912} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f123cb-1b6e-5516-8f83-9ae4f165c4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117913} {:answer/id #uuid "083c3319-bcc0-58ab-a572-803e269dba91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117914} {:answer/id #uuid "61a3cb47-4fd7-510f-bb93-caa965c4b0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2f123cb-1b6e-5516-8f83-9ae4f165c4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117913} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bed7300-6781-5205-97ba-d0b28838a6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117910}] :question/id #uuid "96d2a067-654d-55aa-b5c4-b48e4bab8c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34202" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t) = ⟨t - \\sin t, 1 - \\cos t⟩$ at the point where $t = π$."}]}
